WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.020
In embryonic rodent cortex, radial glia make most cortical neurons.

2
00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:12.340
The cells that guide migrating neurons are also their main source.

3
00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:15.080
That changes how we see the scaffold.

4
00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:19.360
A radial glial cell is a progenitor, a cell that still divides.

5
00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:23.320
Its daughters can become neurons while it keeps making more.

6
00:00:24.283 --> 00:00:29.283
How do we know? In mouse, sorted radial glia made neurons in culture.

7
00:00:30.383 --> 00:00:31.623
Fate mapping marked their descendants.

8
00:00:32.683 --> 00:00:35.683
Nearly all cortical projection neurons carried the mark.

9
00:00:36.543 --> 00:00:41.643
In filmed rat slices, radial glia divided and made neurons that moved along the

10
00:00:41.643 --> 00:00:42.643
parent's own fiber.

11
00:00:43.643 --> 00:00:48.243
Dye-filled recordings also tied dividing precursors to radial glial shape.

12
00:00:49.233 --> 00:00:50.753
There are two routes to neuron.

13
00:00:51.653 --> 00:00:55.613
Direct neurogenesis makes another radial glial cell and a neuron.

14
00:00:56.533 --> 00:01:00.953
Indirect neurogenesis makes another radial glial cell and an intermediate

15
00:01:00.953 --> 00:01:05.993
progenitor. That progenitor leaves the ventricle and divides in the subventricular

16
00:01:05.993 --> 00:01:08.893
zone, usually into two neurons in rodents.

17
00:01:09.893 --> 00:01:13.073
This adds neurons without spending the radial glial cell.

18
00:01:13.073 --> 00:01:18.613
Human cortex also has many outer radial glia, which feed further progenitors.

19
00:01:19.617 --> 00:01:24.637
These progenitors touch two niches, local settings that help keep a stem cell a stem

20
00:01:24.637 --> 00:01:28.397
cell. The apical foot faces cerebrospinal fluid.

21
00:01:29.237 --> 00:01:32.357
In mouse, growth factors in that fluid support proliferation.

22
00:01:33.377 --> 00:01:36.537
The basal foot rests on basement membrane under the pia.

23
00:01:37.377 --> 00:01:39.897
A daughter that leaves loses apical contact.

24
00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:44.680
Neuron output also depends on how many daughters keep dividing.

25
00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:50.560
Call that fraction P, and the fraction that quits Q. P plus Q equals 1.

26
00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:54.080
Below Q = 0.5, the pool grows.

27
00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:56.900
At 0.5, it holds steady.

28
00:01:57.980 --> 00:01:58.980
Above it, it shrinks.

29
00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:04.120
An intermediate progenitor still counts in P. In mouse cortex, the cell cycle

30
00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:05.940
lengthens, mostly in G1.

31
00:02:06.950 --> 00:02:11.010
Inside an apical progenitor, the nucleus moves with that cycle.

32
00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:15.810
Picture a lift. The shaft is the cell; the car is the nucleus.

33
00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:18.310
In G1, it moves basally.

34
00:02:19.310 --> 00:02:22.410
S phase copies DNA in the basal ventricular zone.

35
00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:25.150
In G2, it returns apically.

36
00:02:26.210 --> 00:02:27.370
Mitosis happens there.

37
00:02:28.370 --> 00:02:31.630
Nuclei at different heights make a single cell layer look layered.

38
00:02:32.650 --> 00:02:33.970
Keep the main finding.

39
00:02:34.850 --> 00:02:40.310
In embryonic rodent cortex, radial glia both guide migrating neurons and make most

40
00:02:40.310 --> 00:02:40.650
of them.
